WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.200
Hi everyone. I told you I want to make for my son Lazar a series of illustrations with the forest animals he loves most.

2
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:15.300
And the first in this series was this one — with a mama doe and a fawn.

3
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:22.100
There's a video about it on my channel, if you haven't seen it, take a look. If you're interested, there'll be a link here.

4
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:29.100
Everything went great there, I was pleased when I finished.

5
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:52.500
The second illustration in the series is this bunny. He's finally done. Now I'm pleased with how he came out, but while working I wasn't pleased, because I had two big failures, and this is already the third attempt to draw this illustration.

6
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:04.900
If you remember, if you watched one of my earlier videos — I think the one where I draw breakfast — there I said I was upset about one illustration

7
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:17.500
and that I set it aside to cool off a bit. This is the very one that didn't want things to go smoothly at all.

8
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:26.100
So I took a bit of a break from it and now I've finally come back to it, and at the same time, while you watch this video, I'll show where I went wrong.

9
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:43.500
I don't want this channel to be only "look how beautifully I draw," but to show some of my failures and mistakes too. I'll show you everything, because why should you learn only from your own mistakes when you can learn from mine too.

10
00:01:43.500 --> 00:02:01.700
Anyway, while we're talking about it, I'll play here a video where I draw this illustration. I made it digitally: first I made my mini sketches, I had a clear idea of what I wanted to do.

11
00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:16.200
I found inspiration for this illustration in this book, this picture book that Laza loves very much — "Ten Little Indians." You can see the book was much loved.

12
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:34.000
He inherited this book from his dad and aunt, who, I think, also inherited it from their older sisters. It's a 1973 edition, so the book has somehow managed to survive.

13
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:50.200
And Laza loves to leaf through it and point out the animals, birds, little Indians. It's really full of beautiful illustrations, all done in watercolor, and there are animals too.

14
00:02:50.200 --> 00:03:04.900
And when we get to one illustration, I'll find it now, this one, let's set aside the fact that this lady stops her car and calls the little Indians to get into her car.

15
00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:11.500
Kids, don't get into a car with strangers. We'll skip that part.

16
00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:22.400
Here there's a little bunny hiding in a hollow, and when we get to this illustration, Laza giggles and laughs that this bunny hid there.

17
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:36.200
That's why I wanted to draw him a bunny in a hollow too, and drew him lying in the hollow

18
00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:52.600
with flowers around him, leaves, clover, dandelions, because Laza is currently obsessed with clover and flowers, especially dandelions,

19
00:03:52.600 --> 00:04:04.700
and it's always like that when we go outside, he picks some dandelion for mommy, but never wants to give it to me, instead he clutches it in his hand all the time, doesn't let go and walks around, does whatever comes to mind, but doesn't let go of the flower.

20
00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:12.800
So this bunny is surrounded by burdocks, flowers and clover, and Laza likes that a lot.

21
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:18.100
I showed it to him, he's thrilled, and that makes me very happy.

22
00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:26.600
So, now let's move on to you not seeing me anymore, let's move on to the voice-over, and I'll show you

23
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:29.000
what went wrong for me.

24
00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:41.200
The paper I chose for this illustration is Fabriano Mixed Media. It's paper for mixed media. I also used it for the illustration with the owls

25
00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:53.000
and wanted to use it for the whole series of these forest animals that I plan to make. The paper has a weak texture, so a ballpoint pen behaves well on it.

26
00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:17.300
I drew the lines with a ballpoint pen, masked the places I didn't want to paint when doing the background, and started with the background. First I had to do the sky, so I wet the paper with water so the paints I'd use for the sky would flow nicely into one another.

27
00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:26.700
I used indigo and ocher, and the sky came out fine, the paints blended beautifully.

28
00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:37.800
And then, instead of waiting for the paper to dry completely, I couldn't hold out, I lacked patience, and I started painting trees. I don't know why.

29
00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:49.400
Even though I knew I wanted tree silhouettes against the background, not everything blurring like this, I don't know why I started doing it on wet paper.

30
00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:57.900
Because that's exactly what happened: the paints spread and started merging with the sky.

31
00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:07.900
And on top of everything, these trees that are slightly closer, the big tree on the left, came out like this, with these blotches, so that every brushstroke was visible.

32
00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:26.900
And as if that wasn't enough, when I removed the masking tape, I saw that water had seeped through, that paint had gotten onto these edges that were supposed to stay white, and everything was so ruined that I decided to start over.

33
00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:37.400
And then I again drew the lines with the ballpoint pen, again masked everything that should stay unpainted,

34
00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:56.600
and again wet the background to do the sky, did the sky and this time told myself: "Tanja, wait, let everything dry well so the trees turn out beautiful, so that you have beautiful tree silhouettes against the background." And it went fine with this little tree in the background, the farthest one.

35
00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:05.400
The one on the right, I saw it wasn't very good, but told myself: okay, it'll do. But when I did the one on the left,

36
00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:12.700
then I realized that this struggle simply isn't over and this is definitely not the last attempt.

37
00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:24.300
What's the problem here? One problem is that this isn't watercolor paper, another is that this brush

38
00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:42.000
can't hold enough water for this tree to be done in one stroke. That's how I painted it, surfaces on this paper dry unevenly, and so every puddle of water is visible.

39
00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:56.500
So I got upset again and said: okay, I can't do this a third time now, I'll set it aside, and when I figure out what and how, I'll come back to this illustration, and for now I'll do something else.

40
00:07:56.500 --> 00:08:17.700
At first I should have changed the paper when I made this illustration, but I thought: I've already started one illustration on this paper, I should do everything on it, and that postponed my decision to start this illustration over.

41
00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:37.000
But then I sat down and told myself: Tanja, you're doing this for a son who is still a baby, and he definitely won't say: "Oh, mommy, why did you draw these illustrations on different paper, look what that looks like, I don't want such inconsistency in my room."

42
00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:59.200
That is, really, I don't know where my head was and what I was thinking, but then I decided to change the paper, and when I saw that Baohong paper behaves well in the badger illustration and how evenly it absorbs paint, I decided to work on it.

43
00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:21.500
And I again made the lines on it and masked everything, and when I started doing the sky the same way, everything absorbed beautifully and evenly, and when I started doing the trees, it was a pleasure, because everything really dried well and evenly, the silhouettes came out nicely, I truly enjoyed it.

44
00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:31.600
The next large surface I painted was this trunk and hollow, because I like to work from large surfaces to small details.

45
00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:55.400
And the trunk came out well, especially the bark, you'll see I added the bark details a bit later, it came out wonderfully, and this paper is simply made for trees, landscapes and all watercolor transitions, truly I'd recommend this paper to anyone who likes to paint landscapes and nature.

46
00:09:55.400 --> 00:10:16.700
However, when it comes to illustrations with very many small details, like here, I think this paper isn't the best choice, because it has a very rough texture, and I realized this when I was drawing the fur on the bunny.

47
00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:33.400
Already when I started the underpainting, I understood the fur wouldn't turn out the way I'm used to: finely pulling out these strands so that the hairs and details are nicely visible.

48
00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:40.400
The texture here is so rough that even with the thinnest brush I couldn't pull it out that finely.

49
00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:59.500
But it's fine. I did the underpainting with ultramarine and indigo, because I like the base to be warmer colors, so the cool ones then come through on top and create these shimmers in the fur.

50
00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:06.400
Because you never see a single color on fur, there are always shimmers, shades, changes in color temperature,

51
00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:11.000
and this is where that underpainting matters most.

52
00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:21.600
So there, the bunny came out as he came out, not that bad, could be better, and next time we'll try harder, but there, now he came out like this.

53
00:12:53.600 --> 00:13:00.400
When I moved on to the flowers and leaves, I realized that this struggle would go on.

54
00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:27.700
On these leaves where I had masking fluid, I noticed that the paint, as soon as I started applying it, slightly peeled away from the paper, as if the mask had left some light greasy layer, a film that slightly repels paint, and I had to go over it twice.

55
00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:41.400
It still came out a bit grainy, and the ballpoint lines got a bit lost there, so I decided to emphasize and thicken these lines with a fineliner.

56
00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:52.800
I used a dark gray Unipin fineliner with 0.1 thickness, slightly emphasized these lines and managed to bring the leaves and flowers to the foreground.

57
00:13:52.800 --> 00:14:06.600
The things below in the grass, leaves and flowers, didn't give me as much trouble because there was no mask there. I only struggled a bit because of this texture,

58
00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:15.300
which is so rough that the thin brush catches on it, but fine, I managed.

59
00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:22.100
This struggle, I hope, isn't that noticeable. And when I finished the whole illustration, I stopped and looked,

60
00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:33.200
and I didn't like it, I'd looked at it too long and struggled with it so much, and then I photographed it and sent it to friends, colleagues,

61
00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:44.500
and one of them told me: well, okay, it's not that bad, I like it, just add a bit of contrast, darken the hollow behind the bunny a little,

62
00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:49.900
so that he comes forward a bit more, add some shadows to him and so on.

63
00:14:49.900 --> 00:15:11.100
And I listened and did it, and when I sent it again: there, super, now that's it, now add a ladybug too. And I added a ladybug, and I think it's a great cherry on top, and at the top I added these little birds in the sky and said: that's the end, I'm not touching it anymore.

64
00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:33.700
I finished, we won't look at each other anymore, and when a couple of days later I looked at it with fresh eyes, it no longer seemed so bad to me, it's good, and when I showed it to Laza, he liked it, and that became a sign for me that in the end everything worked out and the effort was worth it.

65
00:15:33.700 --> 00:16:13.700
And here we are at the end of the video, the illustration is done, thank God, and now, as I'm editing the video and looking at where I went wrong, how and why and how I got upset, I'm glad I made it to the end and finished it, and I definitely learned a lot from my mistakes.

66
00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:22.500
I hope you did too, and that you enjoyed this video and would like to watch more videos like it.

67
00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:34.400
If so, give it a like, it means a lot to me, subscribe to the channel and hit the bell to find out when I post a new video, and then I'll see you in that new video.

68
00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:36.300
Well, that's all, bye until then.